WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.002
-Our next guest is an Emmy-
nominated actor and musician.

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:04.337
He stars in the series
"Big Shot"

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:06.172
which is streaming now
on Disney+.

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:09.876
He also hosts the podcast
"Snatching Sinatra" on Wondery,

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:11.077
which is available now.

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:12.912
Please welcome back to the show
John Stamos.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.514
How you are, John?

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:16.181
-Hello, Seth. Nice to see you.

00:00:16.181 --> 00:00:17.650
-It's very exciting news.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.154
You are a new father since
the last time we saw each other.

00:00:21.154 --> 00:00:23.056
-Yes. And I was --

00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:24.724
We were inspired by --
You had your --

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:27.993
Your baby was born in the lobby
of your apartment building?

00:00:27.993 --> 00:00:30.163
-Yes, but that was
my wife's fault.

00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:32.532
-Yeah, we tried to have our kid
at the bowling alley,

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:35.034
but it was closed.

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:37.337
Got to -- The laundromat
was open, so we went there.

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:38.505
-Oh, that's great.
Yeah, you just --

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:39.738
Ultimately,
you got to find a place

00:00:39.738 --> 00:00:41.540
that will make for a good
anecdote down the line.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:42.809
-Is this my good --

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:45.645
What's the good side
that Kristen and Dax were --

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:47.880
-It's great. I think you're
really good. We've --

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.150
You've have always been known
as having no bad sides, John.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.686
-Well, no.
This is my Rob Lowe side.

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:55.221
-Oh, yeah.
That is very Rob Lowe.

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:57.523
-And this is my Scott Baio side.

00:00:57.523 --> 00:00:59.192
So you choose.

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:00.260
-Rob Lowe, please.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:01.460
[ Laughter ]

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.529
-Seth, may I just say
that I just love you.

00:01:03.529 --> 00:01:05.932
I've been a fan of yours for
a long, long time, as you know.

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:07.500
But during this last
year and a half,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.468
you've just
upped your game so much.

00:01:09.468 --> 00:01:11.604
And it's just -- You were just
the guy to turn to.

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:13.539
And I appreciate
how great and funny,

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:16.543
and I was so happy
that you were on and leading us,

00:01:16.543 --> 00:01:18.677
and thank you. That's all.
-Oh, well, thank you very much.

00:01:18.677 --> 00:01:20.947
I felt incredibly lucky
to have a show like this.

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:22.215
And I also want to thank you,

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:25.118
because I think a lot of us
have turned to podcasts,

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:26.752
and you have a wonderful one

00:01:26.752 --> 00:01:29.322
and a story that I did not
know anything about

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:34.594
that came across your desk,
I should say, in the '80s,

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:36.929
which was about the kidnapping
of Frank Sinatra's son.

00:01:36.929 --> 00:01:39.899
How did you first come to know
about this story?

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:42.668
-Isn't it a crazy story? A lot
of people just don't even --

00:01:42.668 --> 00:01:43.969
Didn't even know it happened.

00:01:43.969 --> 00:01:46.305
I was, through my relationship
with the Beach Boys,

00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:49.642
I met Jan and Dean,
the other surf rock duo.

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:52.278
And I was at a fair,
at the Orange County Fair,

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:54.547
I remember early in the '80s.
And Dean Torrence turned to me,

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:56.182
said, "Say, Stamos,
you produce?"

00:01:56.182 --> 00:01:58.951
I'm like, "Yeah, sure." I didn't
know what producing was.

00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:02.455
He said, "I loaned
the seed money to my best friend

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:04.223
to kidnap Frank Sinatra Jr."

00:02:04.223 --> 00:02:06.126
I was like, "What?"

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:08.127
And he gave me this --
Look at this.

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:09.595
He said that Barry Keenan,

00:02:09.595 --> 00:02:13.766
the mastermind who kidnapped
Sinatra Jr., wrote this in jail

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:15.367
and gave it to Dean,
said, "I have the rights,

00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:16.702
if you want
to do something with it."

00:02:16.702 --> 00:02:18.404
I didn't really know
what to do with it back then.

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:19.972
And a few years later,
I was like,

00:02:19.972 --> 00:02:23.142
"Dean, what happened
to that story of your friend?"

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:25.244
And he said they tried to get it
made for many, many years,

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:28.214
but Sinatra didn't want it made.
He didn't want it talked about.

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:30.116
In fact, Barry talks about
in the podcast,

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:33.786
he talks about that Sinatra put
a hit on him about three times.

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:35.421
And it went on
for like 50 years.

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:39.191
Finally, these guys were so old,
the hit man Barry talks about --

00:02:39.191 --> 00:02:40.793
The hit man
had him in his sights,

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:44.330
and then his colostomy bag
broke, and he missed him,

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:47.833
which I think in the mob,
that's a rite of passage, right,

00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:50.403
like, to get to that age
where you have a colostomy bag?

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:51.604
-Because, yeah, it's very hard

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:53.072
to have a long career
in the mob.

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:55.775
So if you can get
to the colostomy bag stage,

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:57.577
that's an accomplishment.
But this is so --

00:02:57.577 --> 00:03:00.412
-It's a crazy story.
I mean, it's --

00:03:00.412 --> 00:03:02.248
I always pitch it
as, like, you know,

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:04.450
the Marx Brothers meets
the Coen Brothers or something.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.051
But, truthfully, it's about a --

00:03:06.051 --> 00:03:07.787
You know, it's about
a mentally ill man.

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:10.790
And being able to do it as a
podcast, it really had the time

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:12.525
to sort of dig into his psyche

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:14.960
and try to figure out
why he did this.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.363
He was at his lowest,
this guy Barry Keenan.

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:19.232
And he was overlooking
Catalina Island,

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:22.402
and he said God --
a voice came over on the radio

00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:24.103
and told him to kidnap somebody.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:25.971
And then he said the radio
wasn't even on.

00:03:25.971 --> 00:03:29.108
And his first choice
was going to be Bob Hope's son,

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:30.909
but he thought,
"Oh, that's too patriotic.

00:03:30.909 --> 00:03:33.045
I should go for Sinatra's,
you know, kid

00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:36.315
because, you know, the mob
and Sinatra's --" you know.

00:03:36.315 --> 00:03:38.551
So, the third time he --

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:41.688
His third attempt was the day
that Kennedy got assassinated.

00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:43.756
So the whole nation's
like this, right?

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:44.890
Sinatra's like this.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:46.925
You know his relationship
with the Kennedys.

00:03:46.925 --> 00:03:49.795
And he gets up -- He said,
"Well, that's in Dallas.

00:03:49.795 --> 00:03:51.363
I got a schedule to keep."

00:03:51.363 --> 00:03:53.399
He gets up --
He gets up to Tahoe,

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:56.702
gets Sinatra Jr.,
brings him down the hill,

00:03:56.702 --> 00:03:58.737
stashes him
in the valley over here,

00:03:58.737 --> 00:04:01.574
and then realizes,
"Oh, man, I left the gun.

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:03.542
I left fingerprints.
I left my fake mustaches.

00:04:03.542 --> 00:04:04.944
I got to go back up."

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:07.346
So he asks his girlfriend,
who was a virgin at the time,

00:04:07.346 --> 00:04:08.715
to drive him back up.

00:04:08.715 --> 00:04:11.083
She gets so turned on,
she has sex with him

00:04:11.083 --> 00:04:12.885
when they get there
overlooking the crime scene.

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:14.520
-Sure, who wouldn't? Yeah.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.922
-And he had --
Right, that happens.

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:17.089
-Yeah.
-And he had to --

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:18.357
You know, the FBI was onto him,

00:04:18.357 --> 00:04:21.360
so they disguise themselves
as honeymoon skiers.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:22.562
But in a blink --

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:24.330
At this moment, he's got --

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.933
He's the most wanted man
in America.

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.969
He's got the FBI,
the CIA, J. Edgar Hoover.

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:32.104
Everyone wanted to --
Sam Giancana called Sinatra

00:04:32.104 --> 00:04:34.273
and said, "Let me do it
my way, Frank."

00:04:34.273 --> 00:04:37.910
And he's going down
the bunny slopes in Lake Tahoe.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.479
So...
-It is a crazy, crazy story.

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:42.815
And then I guess at the time,

00:04:42.815 --> 00:04:44.884
Frank Sinatra
probably didn't know

00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:46.485
that you were maybe one day
going to tell this story,

00:04:46.485 --> 00:04:48.220
but you got to meet him.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:49.621
-Yeah.
-This is you and Paula Abdul

00:04:49.621 --> 00:04:52.125
and Frank Sinatra.
Where did you see him?

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:55.428
-This was in Orange County,
and I got to bring my family.

00:04:55.428 --> 00:04:56.895
I think -- We got to meet him.

00:04:56.895 --> 00:05:00.132
I don't think -- Frank was
more of a "Straight Up" --

00:05:00.132 --> 00:05:02.201
He was more of a Paula Abdul fan
than mine.

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:06.738
But we called ahead
to see if we could meet him.

00:05:06.738 --> 00:05:08.507
And they said, "Well,
if Sinatra wants to meet you,

00:05:08.507 --> 00:05:09.742
he'll let you know."

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:11.277
What does that mean?

00:05:11.277 --> 00:05:13.312
So, Rickles goes on, and
I didn't know him at the time.

00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:15.615
You know, since, I was
very good friends with him,

00:05:15.615 --> 00:05:18.985
as you might know,
over the last 15 years.

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:22.355
So, Rickles goes on,
and then it's intermission.

00:05:22.355 --> 00:05:24.556
And someone says,
"Mr. Sinatra will meet you now."

00:05:24.556 --> 00:05:26.826
So we get to go back. And my dad
was the biggest Sinatra fan,

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:28.828
so it was a really important
moment for me.

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:31.497
We go back, and my mom
had this story for years

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:34.099
that Sinatra tried to hit on her
on her wedding --

00:05:34.099 --> 00:05:35.468
on her honeymoon.

00:05:35.468 --> 00:05:37.636
And she said, "No, Frank,
I'm a married woman," you know?

00:05:37.636 --> 00:05:39.205
So, at this concert she --

00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:42.408
First of all, we get backstage,
and she's into a couple drinks.

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:44.243
She beelines towards Frank.

00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:45.945
Jilly Rizzo,
who was Frank's guy,

00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:48.180
you know, tackles --
grabs my mother.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:49.615
And I'm like,
"Don't kill my mom!

00:05:49.615 --> 00:05:51.551
Let me get a picture of Frank
first, then you can beat her up.

00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:53.619
But let's get the picture."

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:55.254
So it was a pretty,
pretty great moment.

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:59.124
And then at the end of the
concert, Rickles comes back out.

00:05:59.124 --> 00:06:01.594
You know how they introduce
the celebrities in the audience,

00:06:01.594 --> 00:06:03.696
"Oh, Tony Danza, Paula Abdul.

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:04.730
That's John Stamos."

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.231
And I was sort of
embarrassed to stand up.

00:06:06.231 --> 00:06:08.134
And then, he said, "Oh, he's
probably in the cheap seats.

00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:10.202
In fact, he's
probably in the grass.

00:06:10.202 --> 00:06:11.804
In fact, he's probably
smoking grass."

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:13.605
And Sinatra grabs the mic
from Rickles

00:06:13.605 --> 00:06:16.542
and says,
"Smoke one for me, Johnny."

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:17.844
And I was like, "There we go."

00:06:17.844 --> 00:06:19.812
[ Laughter ]

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:22.481
-I also want to talk
about "Big Shot."

00:06:22.481 --> 00:06:23.715
This is really exciting.

00:06:23.715 --> 00:06:27.253
So, you play sort of
a Bobby Knight-esque coach

00:06:27.253 --> 00:06:29.121
who gets kicked out of the NCAA,

00:06:29.121 --> 00:06:30.890
goes to coach
an all-girls school.

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:32.658
And one of the really exciting
things about this

00:06:32.658 --> 00:06:37.029
is you've been getting very good
critical response to this show,

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:38.297
good critical response

00:06:38.297 --> 00:06:40.132
specifically to
your performance.

00:06:40.132 --> 00:06:41.466
And I wanted to bring that up

00:06:41.466 --> 00:06:43.469
because the last time
you were here, we talked about

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:46.872
how that maybe wasn't the case
with "Fuller House"

00:06:46.872 --> 00:06:48.240
and you were kind enough

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:49.908
to let us read you
some of those reviews.

00:06:49.908 --> 00:06:51.910
Let's just remind you
real quick.

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:53.546
"Hollywood Reporter" said,
"It's doubtful

00:06:53.546 --> 00:06:56.648
that there will be
a more painful 2016 TV episode

00:06:56.648 --> 00:06:58.016
than the 'Fuller House' pilot."

00:06:58.016 --> 00:06:59.852
-I mean --
[ Audience oohs ]

00:06:59.852 --> 00:07:01.553
-"There's a point
where nostalgia becomes

00:07:01.553 --> 00:07:03.055
more like necrophilia,

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:05.357
and 'Fuller House'
immediately crosses that line."

00:07:05.357 --> 00:07:06.492
"Netflix's 'Fuller House'

00:07:06.492 --> 00:07:09.028
is like a porn parody
without the porn."

00:07:09.028 --> 00:07:11.030
[ Laughter ]

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:12.664
-That was great!

00:07:12.664 --> 00:07:16.168
That was one of my most favorite
talk show moments ever.

00:07:16.168 --> 00:07:17.870
We were so good together,
you and I.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.438
We had chemistry.
-We had chemistry.

00:07:19.438 --> 00:07:21.573
And, look, you're good
without me, too,

00:07:21.573 --> 00:07:23.742
because I just --
I do want to read some of this.

00:07:23.742 --> 00:07:24.944
"Entertainment Weekly,"

00:07:24.944 --> 00:07:27.613
Kristen Baldwin said,
"Stamos scores

00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:29.047
in heartfelt dramedy 'Big Shot,'

00:07:29.047 --> 00:07:32.285
delivering dry wit and sweet
sentiment in equal measure."

00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:34.720
"Time" magazine,
that's a big one, Judy Berman,

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:36.555
"'Big Shot' a surprisingly
lovable sports drama.

00:07:36.555 --> 00:07:39.058
Stamos reliably likeable

00:07:39.058 --> 00:07:41.093
and makes it easy to root
for a high-strung bully."

00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:43.662
That's great.
"Esquire," another big one,

00:07:43.662 --> 00:07:45.197
Brady Langmann, "Stamos gives

00:07:45.197 --> 00:07:48.133
one of the most heartfelt
performances of his career."

00:07:48.133 --> 00:07:50.102
And -- Oh, son of a gun.

00:07:50.102 --> 00:07:51.837
-What? What happened?

00:07:51.837 --> 00:07:54.140
-One of the "Fuller House" ones
slipped in here.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:55.941
-Uh-oh.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:55.941 --> 00:07:58.276
-It's "New York Times,"
James Poniewozik,

00:07:58.276 --> 00:08:01.446
"The series begins
as a sitcom family reunion.

00:08:01.446 --> 00:08:03.282
It becomes
a self-conscious, dated,

00:08:03.282 --> 00:08:05.985
and maudlin reminder
of the ceaseless march of time

00:08:05.985 --> 00:08:08.553
in your inevitable demise."

00:08:08.553 --> 00:08:09.888
-So he liked it then?

00:08:09.888 --> 00:08:12.657
-I think he liked it, yeah.
We only have that part.

00:08:12.657 --> 00:08:16.428
I'm sure he bounced back.

00:08:16.428 --> 00:08:17.896
-It was very --

00:08:17.896 --> 00:08:19.798
It was alarming, those reviews.

00:08:19.798 --> 00:08:20.899
And they weren't fake, either.

00:08:20.899 --> 00:08:22.101
And then, yeah, then "Big Shot"

00:08:22.101 --> 00:08:23.435
has been just getting
love letters.

00:08:23.435 --> 00:08:25.704
It's a David E. Kelly show,
and I'm so proud of it.

00:08:25.704 --> 00:08:27.539
And then, you know, we're
on the bubble, as they say.

00:08:27.539 --> 00:08:28.874
So, you know, who knows?

00:08:28.874 --> 00:08:30.543
-Alright, well, I hope
you get right off that bubble

00:08:30.543 --> 00:08:33.246
where you -- I hope you get on
the Rob Lowe side of the bubble,

00:08:33.246 --> 00:08:34.981
which is where
we all want you to be.

00:08:34.981 --> 00:08:36.782
And thank you so much, John.

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:38.483
It's always a delight
to see you.

00:08:38.483 --> 00:08:40.720
-Thank you, Seth. And thank you
very much for having me.

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:42.922
-"Big Shot" is
streaming now on Disney+,

00:08:42.922 --> 00:08:45.557
and "Snatching Sinatra"
is available on Wondery

00:08:45.557 --> 00:08:47.059
and wherever you get your
podcasts.

